{
  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_051 (multimodal, singleprofile, scgpt+resnet50, concat) achieved 0.4275 acc vs config_018 (rna_only, singleprofile, scgpt) at 0.4106, suggesting a modest multimodal benefit. However, with only two data points, we have no information on whether multiprofile adds value over singleprofile—a decision that affects roughly half the search space. Question: Does multiprofile (with the simplest aggregation, avgpool) improve over singleprofile when all other factors (scgpt RNA encoder, resnet50 image encoder, concat fusion) are held constant? Why this is the best next experiment: config_002 is the most controlled comparison to config_051, changing only profile_setting (single→multi) and adding the minimally necessary aggregation (avgpool). If multiprofile+avgpool does not clearly beat 0.4275, it strongly suggests multiprofile is not the key lever and we can focus budget on singleprofile variants (fusion, encoders). If it does improve, it validates the multiprofile direction and motivates testing transformer aggregation next. This single experiment reduces the largest remaining architectural uncertainty with minimal confounding variables."
}

